WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.520
Welcome to Grade 7 English, Unit
8: Media, Technology, and News.

2
00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:06.600
Welcome to Unit 8.

3
00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:09.340
This unit is called "Media, Technology,

4
00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:15.980
and News" because you will explore how information travels
in the modern world and why it matters to think critically

5
00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:17.340
about everything you read,

6
00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:18.560
watch, and share.

7
00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:27.700
Every day, millions of messages flow through newspapers,
television channels, websites, and social media platforms.

8
00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:31.580
Some of these messages are carefully researched and truthful.

9
00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:35.880
Others are misleading, exaggerated, or completely false.

10
00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:39.380
As a young person growing up in the digital age,

11
00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:46.700
one of the most important skills you can develop is the
ability to tell the difference between reliable information and

12
00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:47.320
misinformation.

13
00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:53.800
In this short lecture, preview the key
ideas and prepare for the independent lesson.

14
00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:55.900
By the end of this unit,

15
00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:01.780
you will be able to explain what media literacy
means and why it is important for young people today.

16
00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:08.280
you will also identify the key features of
a reliable news source and an unreliable one.

17
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:14.940
you will also read a text about media and answer
comprehension and analysis questions in full sentences.

18
00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:17.660
Keep these goals in mind as you work.

19
00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:26.560
Your vocabulary work begins with these words: express,
claim, allege, correspond, explanation, influence.

20
00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:32.140
Listen to each pronunciation in the
vocabulary lab, study its meaning in context,

21
00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:35.780
and use the word accurately in
your own discussion and writing.

22
00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:44.420
The reading sequence includes Media, Technology, and News:
source text 1 and Media, Technology, and News: source text 2.

23
00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:45.800
Read actively.

24
00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:50.620
Identify each text's central ideas,
notice viewpoint and reliability,

25
00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:54.700
and support every inference with
relevant evidence from the text.

26
00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:58.540
The first language focus is Present Perfect + Just.

27
00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:07.000
What it means and why it matters The present perfect tense
is used to talk about actions or experiences that happened at

28
00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:10.780
an unspecified time in the past but are important now.

29
00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:15.680
When we add just, we emphasise that
something happened very recently.

30
00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:18.400
This is especially useful in news reporting,

31
00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:24.800
where journalists often say things like "The government has
just announced a new policy" to show that the news is fresh

32
00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:25.380
and current.

33
00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:29.360
Understanding this structure will help
you share news and describe recent...

34
00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:35.840
Notice the structure in the model examples, then apply
it deliberately in connected sentences and paragraphs.

35
00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:42.520
In speaking, you will complete Speaking
1: Debate: Digital Media Ethics.

36
00:02:43.190 --> 00:02:48.040
Organise your ideas, use evidence
and respond clearly to questions.

37
00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:51.920
In writing, you will plan, draft, check and revise.

38
00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:55.520
Use the success criteria before submitting your work.

39
00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:56.900
You are ready to begin.

40
00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:03.480
Start with the vocabulary lab, continue through
reading and comprehension, apply the grammar focus,

41
00:03:03.980 --> 00:03:06.200
and complete the speaking and writing practices.

42
00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:12.300
Use AI English for hints and feedback, and
bring your questions to the live sessions.
